---
/**
 * DETAIL ROUTE — /insights/[slug]
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Generated from the shared data files, so adding an article creates its route
 * automatically — no new page file needed.
 *
 * TWO KINDS OF PAGE, ONE ROUTE
 *   A slug with a written body renders it (`InsightArticlePage`); a slug without
 *   one renders the reservation shell (`ReservationPage`). The branch is driven
 *   by `insightPageFor()`, so writing an article for real is: write its body
 *   file, add it to `insightDetailPages` in `src/data/insight-detail.js`, and
 *   let its path be subtracted from `detailRoutePaths` — which that registry's
 *   `builtInsightPaths` does automatically, so the noindex entry and the sitemap
 *   entry can never disagree.
 *
 *   Article 01 (`how-to-find-reliable-suppliers-in-china`) is published. The
 *   other eleven still render the reservation shell, which is kept rather than
 *   deleted for exactly that reason: the twelve routes were published before any
 *   content existed and every link to them is live, so an article must resolve
 *   to something real the moment its body is added.
 *
 * WHY NOT ONE FILE PER ARTICLE
 *   A static `src/pages/insights/<slug>.astro` would also work, but then the
 *   route list lives in two places (the file system and the data registry) and
 *   the reservation fallback has to be duplicated for every article still to be
 *   written. One route, two branches, one registry — the same architecture
 *   `src/pages/industries/[slug].astro` and `src/pages/services/[slug].astro`
 *   use.
 *
 * THE COST OF ONE ROUTE FOR TWO BRANCHES — measured, and the same on all three
 *   Because both shells are imported statically, Astro links BOTH branches'
 *   stylesheets on EVERY `/insights/<slug>` page, so a written article ships
 *   `inner-pages.css` (the reservation shell's sheet) without using it, and a
 *   reserved article ships `insight-article.css`. That is a small amount of CSS
 *   the visitor has almost certainly downloaded already — `inner-pages.css` is
 *   also the sheet for `/insights`, `/industries` and `/case-studies`. The
 *   alternative is the same path in two route tables, where Astro's
 *   static-over-dynamic precedence would decide the winner silently.
 *
 * INDEXABILITY — THE ONE STEP TO REMEMBER
 *   The reservation branch is `noindex` and absent from sitemap.xml. A written
 *   article is indexable: its path is subtracted from `detailRoutePaths` in
 *   `src/data/routes.js` through `builtInsightPaths`.
 *
 *   ⚠ Forgetting that subtraction fails SILENTLY. The article renders, every
 *   internal link to it works, the copy is complete — and it is simply absent
 *   from search. `npm run audit` fails only when the two signals CONTRADICT
 *   ("noindex but listed in the sitemap"); a page that is noindex AND unlisted
 *   is a perfectly consistent pair, so nothing complains. Adding the slug to
 *   `insightDetailPages` is what does both halves at once.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */
import ReservationPage from '../../components/ReservationPage.astro';
import InsightArticlePage from '../../components/insights/InsightArticlePage.astro';

import { insightPageFor } from '../../data/insight-detail.js';
import { detailRouteGroups, reservationForDetail } from '../../data/routes.js';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return detailRouteGroups['insights'].map((entry) => {
    const content = insightPageFor(entry.slug);

    return {
      params: { slug: entry.slug },
      props: content
        ? { content, article: entry.item, parent: entry.parent }
        : {
            content: null,
            page: reservationForDetail(entry.kind, entry.item, entry.parent),
          },
    };
  });
}

const { content, article, parent, page } = Astro.props;
---

{
  content ? (
    <InsightArticlePage article={article} content={content} parent={parent} />
  ) : (
    <ReservationPage {...page} />
  )
}
